scher around one-in-four and raters call the open seat Lean Republican. Kansas's eight-point Republican lean without Kelly on the ballot supports a modest but real Republican edge.", "watch": "If independent polling confirms the late-October tie rather than the September deficits, the race would tighten toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Laura Kelly (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kansas is trending toward Republicans in the open race to succeed term-limited Governor Laura Kelly. Ty Masterson leads Cindy Holscher by roughly three to four points across recent polls, though a Democratic-sponsored survey in late October showed the race tied. Independent polls from September had Masterson ahead by three to seven points. Eight race raters call the contest Lean Republican or worse for Democrats, with only one calling it a Tossup. Prediction markets give Holscher roughly a one-in-four chance.\n\nPresident Trump campaigned for Masterson and affirmed his endorsement days before the election, and attack advertising has dominated the airwaves. Kansas leans eight points Republican at the presidential level, and Kelly's absence removes the incumbent advantage that carried Democrats to two wins here. Holscher needs to consolidate her party and win moderates in the Kansas City suburbs to close the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1817, "margin": -4.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.161, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1643, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1648, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1797, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1739, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.215, "margin": -4.3}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2315, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2051, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2039, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2527, "margin": -4.2}, {"date": "